    {"ref": 2, "quote": "To deal with the limitations of finite-state chunking, the apertium-recursive module (Swanson et al. 2021) was developed by Daniel Swanson as part of Google Summer of Code 20193 to apply structural transfer rules recursively using contextfree grammars (CFGs) and a Generalized Left-right Right-reduce (GLR) parser."},
    {"ref": 2, "quote": "the word order of a set of nested prepositional phrases needs to be completely reversed (or in linguistic terms, the order of noun phrases (NPs) and adpositional phrases (PPs) each needs to be reversed), regardless of the number of prepositional phrases involved in order to translate from English to Basque."}
  ],
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "The apertium-recursive module was developed to address limitations of finite-state chunking by enabling recursive structural transfer using context-free grammars (CFGs) and a GLR parser [2]. It works by reversing the word order of nested prepositional phrases during translation, such as flipping the order of noun phrases (NPs) and adpositional phrases (PPs) when translating English to Basque [2].",
  "clarification": ""
}

