    {"ref": 2, "quote": "If you wanted to eat 150 calories of raisins, you’d be able to eat approximately 50 grams. Yet the same number of calories would give you almost 220 grams of grapes — more than four times as much."}, {"ref": 2, "quote": "The main benefit of lowering the energy density of your diet is that you end up eating less, without actually realizing it."}, {"ref": 3, "quote": "Frozen grapes have an energy density of just 0.7... I take around 120 grams... to satisfy my sweet tooth without causing me to go overboard on the calorie."}, {"ref": 4, "quote": "Most fresh fruits and vegetables have a low energy density."}
  ],
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "Grapes are used to explain energy density by showing how lower energy density foods (like grapes) allow you to eat more volume for fewer calories [2], and how this leads to eating less without noticing [2]. In the author's diet, frozen grapes are used as a dessert alternative with low energy density (0.7) to satisfy sweet cravings without overeating [3]. They also contribute phytochemicals not found in desserts like ice cream [3]. Most fresh fruits and vegetables, including grapes, are categorized as low-energy-density foods [4].",
  "clarification": ""
}

